<h2 id="why-use-them">Why use them <a class="heading-anchor" href="#why-use-them" aria-label="Permalink to Why use them">#</a></h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>They sound like themselves.</strong> Voice rules, address terms and guardrails are part of the repository, not a one-line prompt.</li>
  <li><strong>They share your canon.</strong> The Grimoire gives them the same books, lines and items you can look up.</li>
  <li><strong>They can check a quote.</strong> The archive's <code>verify</code> command is part of the workflow.</li>
  <li><strong>They can use MCP.</strong> Connect the MCP server and the persona can search the archive as a tool.</li>
  <li><strong>They can ask each other.</strong> A read-only consult copy lets one character ask another a question without sharing private notes.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="how-they-fit">How they fit together <a class="heading-anchor" href="#how-they-fit" aria-label="Permalink to How they fit together">#</a></h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Grimoire</strong> — the archive both personas read.</li>
  <li><strong>MCP server</strong> — the optional tool bridge that lets a client search it directly.</li>
  <li><strong>Personas</strong> — the voice and behaviour layer on top.</li>
</ul>

<p>You can install one persona and skip the other. You can use the Grimoire without either. The pieces are designed to be adopted separately.</p>

<h2 id="for-experts">For experts <a class="heading-anchor" href="#for-experts" aria-label="Permalink to For experts">#</a></h2>

<p class="muted">How the personas are kept apart, how consult calls work, and what
the tests cover.</p>

<h3 id="shared-archive">Separate lives</h3>

<p>Each agent has its own repository, entry file, private memory and audit. Neither
agent imports the other's files. What they share is the Grimoire, reached through
a <code>lore/</code> link or clone.</p>

<p>That separation is on purpose. One agent's private notes and voice should not
leak into the other, and either agent should be deployable on its own.</p>

<h3 id="asking-a-friend">The consult</h3>

<p>They can still work together. <code>series/agents.json</code> lists the agents
in the series. In the OpenCode deployment, the sync reads that list and installs
two things per agent: the main entry, and a read-only consult copy. OpenClaw uses
the same list, but its consult entries are added in configuration.</p>

<p>A main agent can ask a peer's consult copy a question. In OpenCode that is the
subagent tool; in OpenClaw it is <code>sessions_spawn</code> with the peer's
<code>-consult</code> id. The copy reads the Grimoire and answers in character,
but cannot edit files, run shell commands or ask someone else. In OpenCode it
also cannot read the peer's private folder. Its reply is lore to think about, not
instructions to follow.</p>

<h3 id="tests">The tests</h3>

<p>Each agent repository ships its own audit. The Drifter currently passes 20/20
suites and Eris 15/15. The suites cover voice, quotes, module completeness, the
conversation path and the shared structure gates.</p>

